}</style></head><body><main class="lab"><header><p class="kicker">Computer science (graph search), Form 4 and 5</p><h1>Hops in a knowledge graph</h1><p class="try"><strong>Try this:</strong> Drag the round handle at the bottom to the right to follow one link, then two links, away from the question.</p></header><div class="top"><section class="card idea"><h2>Key idea</h2><p>Your question is not physics, so this lab uses the closest science a student can explore: computer science. A knowledge graph stores facts as dots joined by links. Following one link at a time moves the search one hop further from the question, so an answer two hops away appears only after two hops.</p><p>Facts reached after h hops = facts at distance 0 + distance 1 + ... + distance h</p></section><section class="card predict"><h2>Predict first</h2><p>The question is &quot;Which country is the city of the Petronas Towers in?&quot; The graph starts at the dot &quot;Petronas Towers&quot;. The answer, Malaysia, is not linked directly to it. How many hops are needed to reach Malaysia?</p><ul><li>1 hop</li><li>2 hops</li><li>3 hops</li></ul><details><summary>Check your prediction</summary><p>Drag the handle one step at a time and watch when the Malaysia dot turns coloured and &quot;Answer found&quot; changes.</p></details></section></div><section class="card steps"><h2>Steps</h2><ol><li>Leave the handle at 0 hops. Only the question dot is coloured, so nothing else is known yet.</li><li>Drag the handle to 1 hop. Watch the three dots linked straight to the question turn coloured, and read how many facts are reached.</li><li>Drag the handle to 2 hops. Watch which dot is reached through Kuala Lumpur, and see how many extra facts appear that are not the answer.</li></ol></section><section class="benchrow" aria-label="The lab bench"><div class="bench"><svg id="bench-art" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-label="Knowledge graph, 0 hops followed, only the question is reached">
<line class="edge" id="e-kl" x1="55" y1="125" x2="190" y2="50"/>
